---
import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';

/**
 * SEO / positioning page (research Part 12 P7, spec section 4 of badger-fit
 * docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-18-m-web-4-orphaned-pages.md). Part 7 found
 * that the privacy demand people actually type is "no subscription" and "no
 * account", which is why this page leads with money rather than with privacy.
 *
 * HONEST ROUNDUP, binding: the competitor table describes shapes (subscription,
 * capped free tier) and never prices, because prices go stale and a wrong number
 * about someone else's product is worse than no number. Each entry says what is
 * good about the app as well as what it costs. The caveat paragraph and the
 * checked-on date under the table are not decoration: they are what makes the
 * table honest six months from now. Re-check the date when you touch a row.
 *
 * Source for the competitor rows: badger-fit
 * docs/competitive_research_2026-07.md Parts 5 and 9 (store listings and public
 * review corpora, gathered 2026-07-13).
 *
 * Badger's own claims were checked against the app source and pubspec on
 * 2026-07-25: no billing, ads, or analytics dependency exists in the project.
 * 2026-09-03: still true of the app. The website itself now runs cookieless
 * page-view analytics (GoatCounter); the claim here is scoped to the app and
 * the site's own counting is stated plainly rather than omitted.
 * The "catch" section is deliberately as long as the good news.
 * 2026-09-24 voice pass: the "If free makes you suspicious" section merged into
 * "Why there is nothing to charge for"; its id sits on that paragraph.
 */

const description =
  'Badger is a workout tracker with no subscription, no account, and no caps on routines or history. What free actually means here, where the app falls short, and how the other trackers charge.';

const others = [
  {
    name: 'FitNotes',
    body: 'Genuinely free, no subscription, no ads, Android only. Development runs at roughly a release a year, so it is a stable simple log rather than a growing app. If you are on Android and it does what you need, there is little reason to switch.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Hevy',
    body: 'A well-made app with a generous free tier that is nonetheless capped: a limited number of saved routines, limits on custom exercises, and older measurement history held behind Hevy Pro, which is a subscription. Plenty of people happily pay for it.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Strong',
    body: 'The long-established one. The free tier is limited to a small number of routine templates, and the rest sits behind Strong Pro. It also requires an account to use.',
  },
  {
    name: 'StrongLifts 5x5',
    body: "The program's own app. Now subscription-first: its store listing states the app requires a subscription to use.",
  },
];
---

<HelpLayout title="Workout tracker with no subscription" description={description}>
  <ArticleSchema
    slot="head"
    headline="A workout tracker with no subscription"
    description={description}
    dateModified="2026-09-24"
    about={['Workout tracking', 'Free software']}
  />

  <h1>A workout tracker with no subscription</h1>

  <p>
    Badger is free: no subscription, no account, no ads, and no caps on routines or history. This
    page covers what that includes, where Badger falls short, and how other trackers charge.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-badger-costs">What Badger costs</h2>

  <p>
    Nothing, on Google Play or the App Store, and there is no paid tier. Free here means all five of
    these:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No account.</strong> No sign-up, no login, no email address. Install it and start
      logging.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No subscription and no in-app purchase.</strong> No Pro version, no trial running down,
      and nothing in the app asking you to upgrade.
    </li>
    <li><strong>No ads.</strong></li>
    <li>
      <strong>No caps.</strong> Unlimited routines, history and custom exercises.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Free export.</strong> Every set exports to CSV, the whole database backs up to a single
      file you keep wherever you like, and routines and gym setups share as plain files.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><a href="/promise/">The Badger promise</a> puts these commitments in writing.</p>

  <h2 id="why">Why there is nothing to charge for</h2>

  <p>
    It is arithmetic: Badger has no servers. No account means no user database, no sync engine and no
    storage bill, so there is no monthly cost to recover from you. Your training sits in a database
    on your phone, which is why the app is free and also why it does not sync between devices. The
    same decision produces both.
  </p>

  <p id="suspicious">
    Asking where the money comes from is the right instinct with any free app. Badger is one person's
    project, with no investors, no advertising business and nothing to pay back. The app has no
    analytics, and your training is not held anywhere it could be sold. (This
    website counts page views without cookies; the <a href="/legal/privacy/#website">privacy
    policy</a> lists what it records.)
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-catch">The catch</h2>

  <p>Here is what you give up, so you can decide before you import years of history.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No sync between devices.</strong> One phone at a time. An optional backup to your own
      Google Drive, or a database file you move yourself, carries your log to a new phone, but two
      devices do not stay in step. See <a href="/help/phone-migration/">moving to a new phone</a>.
    </li>
    <li><strong>No web app and no desktop version.</strong> The phone is the whole product.</li>
    <li><strong>No Apple Watch or Wear OS app.</strong> You cannot log a set from your wrist.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>iOS is younger than Android.</strong> Both get every feature, but Android has been in
      people's hands far longer, so it is the more tested of the two. On iOS, Badger is iPhone only;
      there is no iPad version.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No social feed</strong>, no followers, no leaderboards and no streak badges. Some people
      want those.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No AI coach inside the app.</strong> Instead, Badger hands you your own data in formats
      you can give to whichever assistant you already use, with prompts to start from:
      <a href="/guides/ai/">use your own AI</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Support is one person and an email address</strong>, with no guaranteed reply time.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="the-others">How the other trackers charge</h2>

  <p>
    These apps made different choices about money, and for many people one of them is the right pick.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Tracker</th>
          <th>What the free version gets you</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          others.map((o) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <strong>{o.name}</strong>
              </td>
              <td>{o.body}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="fineprint">
    Checked July 2026. Free tiers and prices change often, so read each app's store listing before you
    decide.
  </p>

  <p>
    What matters more than the monthly price is what happens to your history the day you stop paying.
    Where it lives on a company's servers, that is the company's call. In Badger it lives on your phone,
    and in any backup you choose to make.
  </p>

  <h2 id="leaving">If you are coming from one of them</h2>

  <p>
    Badger imports your history from FitNotes, Strong, Hevy and StrongLifts, straight from each app's
    export file, and works out your PRs and graphs from what arrives.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><a href="/guides/switching/">Switching from another app</a> walks through the first five minutes.</li>
    <li>
      <a href="/help/getting-started/#importing">Importing from another app</a> has the per-app export
      steps.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each app has a page on what its export brings across:
      <a href="/guides/fitnotes-alternative/">FitNotes</a>,
      <a href="/guides/strong-alternative/">Strong</a>,
      <a href="/guides/hevy-alternative/">Hevy</a> and
      <a href="/guides/stronglifts-5x5-tracker/">StrongLifts 5x5</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  /* Same wrapper the format page uses: a two-column table fits a phone, but the
     wrapper means a long unbreakable string scrolls inside its own box instead of
     widening the page. Cheap insurance against sideways scroll at 360px. */
  .table-wrap {
    max-width: 100%;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  /* The first column is a name, so it never needs to wrap mid-word and looks
     wrong when it does. The second column takes the rest. */
  .table-wrap td:first-child {
    white-space: nowrap;
    padding-right: 1rem;
  }

  /* Caveats and date-stamps read as fine print, deliberately quieter than the
     table they qualify. --text-muted is AA-checked against the ground. */
  .fineprint {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
